        "title": "4. Integration test gaps (none exist; these are the highest-value additions once Phase 1–4 land)",
        "content": "\n| Gap | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| **`POST /api/runs` → engine `POST /runs` → status polling round-trip** | Not yet built (no `web/app/api/` route handlers exist). Once added: test that web creates the `runs` row *before* calling the engine, and that a failed engine POST still leaves a readable `queued`/`failed` row rather than an orphaned request. |\n| **`engine/main.py execute_run` end-to-end against `SyntheticConnector`** | The one pipeline path that can run without any external API — this should be the first integration test written, using `eval/generate.py` output as fixture data, asserting `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts`/`resolutions` row counts match the ground-truth manifest within tolerance. |\n| **Table-ownership enforcement across the boundary** | No test currently proves the engine *cannot* write `sources`/`rulesets`, or that the web layer *cannot* write `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts`. Given this is called out as a hard invariant three times in AGENTS.md, it deserves an explicit cross-layer test (e.g., a DB role with `INSERT` revoked on those tables per writer, or a query-log assertion). |\n| **Ruleset versioning immutability** | `POST /api/rulesets` \"creates a new version, never mutates\" — no route exists yet, but when it does, needs a test that re-running an old run's export still reflects the ruleset version it was created with, even after a newer version is published. |\n| **Audit log append-only guarantee** | Both layers write to `audit_log`; needs a test (once review-action routes exist) that an `override` action creates a new row rather than mutating the `resolutions` row's history. |\n| **`eval/harness.py` three-system comparison** | `baseline_exact`, `baseline_rules`, `canon_full` are stubbed. Once implemented, needs a smoke test asserting `baseline_exact` never has better precision on structural conflicts than `canon_full` on a fixed seeded dataset — a regression guard on the paper's core claim. |\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
